Architecture heritage
---------------------
The overall architecture — event-based parser, Rowan CST, Salsa
incremental database, HIR and IDE layering — follows the design of
rust-analyzer, which is distributed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
Upstream: https://github.com/rust-analyzer/rust-analyzer

Language heritage
-----------------
BSL and the SDBL query language are part of the 1C:Enterprise platform.
The language itself, its keywords, its syntax and its semantic rules
belong to 1C as the platform author; they are not proprietary to any
third-party reimplementation.

Official references:
  * 1C query language: https://its.1c.ru/db/pubqlang
  * 1C code standards: https://its.1c.ru/db/v8std

Diagnostic rules
----------------
Code-quality diagnostics in crates/ide-diagnostics encode the 1C
code-style standards published at v8std.ru (CC0) and in the official
1C documentation. They do not implement a third party's proprietary
rule set.

Derivation notice for the SDBL and BSL grammar layers
-----------------------------------------------------
The initial grammar work in the following files was written with the
grammar files of the bsl-parser project
(https://github.com/1c-syntax/bsl-parser, LGPL-3.0-or-later) open as
the working reference:

  * crates/lexer/src/sdbl/        (SDBL token inventory)
  * crates/parser/src/grammar/sdbl/  (SDBL grammar)
  * crates/parser/src/grammar/    (BSL grammar layer)
  * crates/lexer/src/lib.rs       (BSL token inventory)

These facts are stated here directly, and not left to be discovered in
the commit history, because the public mirror of this repository does
not carry that history: a disclosure that can only be verified in a
private repository is not a disclosure. The record below is given so
that a reader who has only this file can rely on it.

This is recorded in this repository's own history of December 2025,
though not uniformly per file. The commit that introduced the SDBL lexer
names both upstream grammar files in its commit message and in the file
header. The commit that introduced the SDBL grammar names SDBLParser.g4
in the file header and quotes upstream grammar rules in ANTLR notation in
many of its function doc-comments. For the BSL layer the record is the
planning document shipped in the initial commit, which names the upstream
grammars as the source for both languages; a file header naming
BSLLexer.g4 was added a few commits later. Details and commit identifiers
are in docs/legal/sdbl-provenance-2026-07-audit.md.

The SDBL layer has since been rewritten in bounded slices from official
1C documentation under the clean-room process described in
docs/legal/sdbl-clean-room-slices.md, and now diverges from the upstream
grammar in token inventory, token granularity, rule decomposition and
operator precedence. That rewrite is not finished, and the BSL layer has
not been rewritten at all.

The current assessment — what is proven, what has diverged, and what is
still outstanding — is in docs/legal/sdbl-provenance-2026-07-audit.md.
Until that work completes, the Tier B crates and the final binary remain
under LGPL-3.0-or-later.

Notice for crates written with a copyleft reference open
---------------------------------------------------------
The notice above covers the grammar layers only. Three crates that are
currently licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0 were also written with a
copyleft-licensed project open as the working reference. The three are
not equivalent: the first two inherited structure, the third matched
observable behaviour on purpose and copied nothing. In each case
the annotations saying so were removed from the source in March and
April 2026 without the code being rewritten, so the source as it stands
today carries no trace of this. It is therefore stated here.

  * crates/cfg — the control-flow-graph model was written with the CFG
    package of bsl-language-server (LGPL-3.0-or-later) open, by way of
    an earlier Rust project by the same author. The file headers read
    "Ported from BSL Language Server (Java)" and carried per-method
    "Maps to ... in Java" annotations. The builder architecture is
    independent: a recursive descent over HIR, where the reference uses
    a block writer over a parse tree, and the two share no structural
    device. What follows the reference is the vertex and edge taxonomy,
    its granularity, the decomposition of loops, try/except and labels,
    and the part of the graph API that is not the underlying graph
    library's own vocabulary.

  * crates/bsl-metadata — the crate described itself as "a Rust port of
    the Java mdclasses library" (https://github.com/1c-syntax/mdclasses,
    LGPL-3.0) when it was created, with per-method "Java equivalent"
    annotations. The parsing implementation is independent, and most of
    the model is dictated by the 1C configuration export format itself,
    which is the format this crate has to read. What is inherited is the
    ontology: the set of named types, the split into traits, the builder
    pattern, and part of the method naming.

  * crates/hir-def — several diagnostic ranges and metrics were written
    to reproduce the observable output of bsl-language-server and of the
    ANTLR-generated parser it uses. Those commits changed comments only;
    no code was copied. The behaviour was kept deliberately, so that the
    two tools report the same numbers on the same source.

The licensing tier of crates/cfg and crates/bsl-metadata is under review
in light of the above.

Compatibility with bsl-language-server
--------------------------------------
Where this project reports the same diagnostics as bsl-language-server,
the agreement is deliberate. Rule codes, tags, severities, default
activation and the places a rule fires form an interface that existing
SonarQube quality profiles and CI pipelines depend on; a tool that
reported different rules under different names on the same code would
not replace its predecessor. These values match that project's published
rule documentation, which is where they can be checked.

This is a statement about observable behaviour, not about source code:
implementing the same interface independently is intended, and copying
implementation text is not.

